\Babstract{
This paper explores potential resolutions for NB comments FR02-077 on the C++26 DIS.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

FR02-077 states:
\Bquote{
The syntax is still not in phase with the compromise
that was found in the liaison study group between WG21 and WG14:
attributes are in the wrong place and hurt possible processing in a future C version of contracts.
}

The change would look as follows:

\cppblock{
void f() pre \del{[[attrib]]} (true);  // N5054
void f() pre(true) \ins{[[attrib]]};   // FR02-077
}

That is, the position of the \grammarterm{attribute-specifier-seq}
would move from after \tcode{pre} or \tcode{post}
to after the entire \grammarterm{precondition-specifier} or \grammarterm{postcondition-specifier}.

While this change was not discussed or polled during an SG22 telecon,
it can be considered a \q{compromise} because it increases compatibility
with the in-development C version of contracts in \ref("WG14-N3929"),
with the following C syntax:

\cowel_macro("_Pre"){\cowel_highlight_as("keyword"){_Pre}}

\cppblock{
void f(int n) \_Pre()(n > 0);
// or
#include <stdc_contract.h> // provides #define pre(...) _Pre(__VA_ARGS__)
void f(int n) pre(n > 0);
}

Notably, the current C++ syntax is incompatible with the \tcode{#define pre(...)} approach
because an attribute between \tcode{pre} and the parameter list
would prevent macro substitution.
Earlier revision of \ref("WG14-N3929") actually supported an attribute list
in the same location as C++,
i.e. \tcode{\_Pre [[attrib]] (true)} would be valid,
but this was removed in later revisions.

\h2{Possible solutions}

At this point, there are three plausible solutions to this situation:
\ol{
  \li{
    Keep attributes where they are, as proposed in P2900.
  }
  \li{
    Move attributes to the location suggested in FR02-077,
    i.e. after the \grammarterm{precondition-specifier}.
  }
  \li{
    Remove attributes from contracts entirely for the time being and revisit the issue in C++29.
  }
}

Find below an argument for each of the possible solutions.

\h3{Keep attributes where they are}

The placement of attributes on Contracts
was explored extensively in \ref("P3088R1").
On the subject of the suffix location, it states the following:

\Bquote{
We could consider placing the \grammarterm{attribute-specifier-seq} after the contract assertion:
\cppblock{
bool binary_search(Range r, const T& value)
  pre (is_sorted(r)) [[vendor::audit]];
void f() {
  int i = get_i();
  contract_assert (i > 0) [[vendor::assume]];
  // ...
}
}
While the suffix location seems technically possible — as far as we can tell,
it does not create any outright collisions with existing grammar — it is far from ideal,
in particularly because, just like the prefix location,
it is not obvious to the reader what the attribute appertains to:
\cppblock{
// which pre does the attribute appertain to?
void f(int* i)
  pre(i != nullptr) [[vendor::xxx]] pre (*i > 0);
  // does the attribute appertain to the pre or to the function g as a whole?
void g(int j) pre(j > 0) [[vendor::yyy]];
}
We therefore do not propose the suffix location.
}

Besides this argument of readability,
two extensions for Contracts are already in the pipeline for C++29
which would also make the \tcode{#define} approach incompatible:
\ul{
  \li{
    \ref("P3098R2") \q{Contracts for C++: Postcondition captures} adds:
    \Bindent{\itemdecl{post [old_size = size()] (size() == old_size + 1)}}
    The new \grammarterm{contract-capture-clause} following \tcode{post}
    inhibits macro expansion of \tcode{#define post(...)}.
  }
  \li{
    \ref("P3400R5") \q{Controlling Contract-Assertion Properties} adds:
    \Bindent{\itemdecl{pre<audit>(std::is_sorted(begin, end))}}
    The new \grammarterm{assertion-control-specifier} inhibits macro expansion.
  }
}

WG21 has never agreed to have an approach that is entirely compatible
with the macro-based solution in C.
Doing so would essentially require all future extensions
to go inside the parentheses of \tcode{pre},
which seems undesirable.

Furthermore, C compatibility with the current C++26 syntax is not entirely infeasible.
There at least two possible approaches:
\ul{
  \li{
    Make \tcode{pre} a contextual keyword in C, just like in C++,
    rather than pursuing a \tcode{#define pre(...)} approach.
  }
  \li{
    Instead define \tcode{#define pre _Pre}.
    However, this would affect any use of the identifier,
    including the use of \tcode{pre} as a variable name.
  }
}

Last but not least,
the \ref("WG14-N3929") approach is only
superficially and syntactically compatible with C++ Contracts:
there is no support for ignore semantic,
there is an additional \tcode{\cowel_highlight_as("keyword"){_Inv}} specifier
that combines preconditions and postconditions, etc.
The author of \ref("WG14-N3929") does not seek to maximize C++ compatibility
but vehemently opposes design principles of \ref("P2900R14").
Even if attributes were moved to the proposed location,
C++ Contracts with ignore semantic would remain incompatible.

\Bnote{
Ignored preconditions can still be emulated in C just like for \tcode{assert}:
\cppblock{
#ifdef __cplusplus
// do nothing in C++
#elifdef NDEBUG
#define pre(...) _Pre(true)        // ignore on release
#else
#define pre(...) _Pre(__VA_ARGS__) // enforce on debug
#endif

void f() pre(cond) [[attrib]];
}
Assuming that the suffix syntax for attributes was used in both languages
and that (quick-)enforce semantic is used in C++
this approach is compatible.
It is important that \tcode{pre} does not expand to an empty token sequence
because the following \tcode{[[attrib]]} would otherwise apply to the function.
}

\h3{Move attributes}


\h3{Remove attributes}

Another option is to remove attributes from contracts entirely.
This would give WG21 more time to discuss possible designs and
more time to coordinate with WG14.

While the option is drastic,
there are no standard attributes that motivate attribute usage on contracts.
The only reason \ref("P3088R1") supports attributes
is to allow for vendor-specific attributes,
similar to how \ref("CWG2262") added attributes for \tcode{asm} statements.
Despite \ref("P3088R1") providing extensive motivation
in the form of possible \tcode{[[vendor::audit]]}, \tcode{[[vendor::assume]]}, etc. attributes,
there may not be sufficient \em{urgency} to justify keeping attributes in C++26.

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

\h3(show_number=false){[version.syn]}

Add a feature-test macro to \eelis{version.syn} as follows:

\Bins{
\itemdecl{
#define __cpp_my_new_feature 20XXXXL // \serif{freestanding, also in \tt{<header>}}
}
}

A \tcode{std::vector} is a \exposid{std-vector}.

